  "worked_solution": "First, let's factor the expression as a perfect square trinomial in ${x}^{2}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+10{x}^{2}+25 & ={({x}^{2})}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot ({x}^{2})+{5}^{2} \\\\  & ={({x}^{2}+5)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the sum of squares in the parentheses. To do this, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5 & ={x}^{2}-(-5) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-(5{i}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{5}\\,i)}^{2} \\\\  & =(x+\\sqrt{5}\\,i)(x-\\sqrt{5}\\,i)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+10{x}^{2}+25 & ={({x}^{2}+5)}^{2} \\\\  & ={((x+\\sqrt{5}\\,i)(x-\\sqrt{5}\\,i))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(x+\\sqrt{5}\\,i)}^{2}{(x-\\sqrt{5}\\,i)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
